title:: Novel Oscillators
summary:: Nineteen clean-room experimental oscillator designs
categories:: UGens>Generators

description::

NovelOscUGens is an experimental clean-room collection of oscillator designs
based on published mathematics and standard digital-synthesis techniques. It
contains no extracted firmware, factory wavetable, preset, panel artwork, or
copied product documentation. Each class is audio-rate only and uses a signed
phase model, bounded outputs, finite-value guards, and initialization-time
validation. The v0.2 set adds scanned synthesis, vector phaseshaping,
spectral-frame resynthesis, explicit synchronization laws, state-space
feedback, digital logic, three-dimensional wavetables, and two language-side
oscillator constructors.

subsection:: Series

link::Classes/HarmonicStrideOsc:: evaluates a geometrically weighted harmonic
or fractional-stride series as one closed-form complex sum. It returns sine and
cosine views of the same spectrum.

subsection:: Wavetables and transforms

link::Classes/TableMorph2D:: traverses a user-prepared two-dimensional,
mipmapped wavetable surface.

link::Classes/TableMorph3D:: extends the shared prepared-table backend to a
trilinearly interpolated waveform volume.

link::Classes/SpectralBasisOsc:: shapes coefficients and applies inverse
Fourier, Walsh, Haar, or Daubechies-4 transforms to create an internal
mipmapped waveform.

link::Classes/SpectralFrameBuffer:: validates and loads ratio-and-amplitude
frames for link::Classes/SpectralFrameOsc::, which interpolates them with
independent, phase-continuous partial oscillators.

subsection:: Through-zero and synchronized modulation

link::Classes/TZSplitOsc:: combines true signed phase motion, an asymmetric
BLAMP triangle, nonlinear drive, and even/odd decomposition.

link::Classes/SyncRingOsc:: derives two synchronized modulators from one
carrier and applies two sequential ring-modulation stages.

link::Classes/SyncModeOsc:: applies six explicit phase-event laws to sine,
triangle, saw, or pulse playback.

link::Classes/PMNetworkOsc:: is a three-operator network with independent
row-major PM and through-zero linear-FM matrices.

link::Classes/ClusterPMOsc:: is a four-operator PM network whose operators are
themselves normalized four-component additive clusters.

link::Classes/VectorPhaseOsc:: moves oscillator phase through one to eight
two-dimensional breakpoints.

link::Classes/RippleFormantOsc:: launches two decaying and chirped ripples on
each base-oscillator cycle.

subsection:: Dynamic and feedback systems

link::Classes/ScannedNetworkOsc:: scans a slowly integrated one-dimensional
mass-spring network as a changing waveform.

link::Classes/ShiftLogicOsc:: couples two triangle oscillators through a seeded
binary register and DAC feedback.

link::Classes/QuadratureFeedbackOsc:: integrates a regulated nonlinear
two-state orbit and exposes four signed directions.

subsection:: Large oscillator banks

link::Classes/PartialClusterOsc:: generates a procedurally warped and shaped
additive spectrum with deterministic unison voices.

link::Classes/ScaleSpreadOsc:: distributes a fixed oscillator bank over an
arbitrary ascending ratio scale with continuous crossfades between degrees.

link::Classes/RatioFamilyOsc:: builds a phase-shaped family from a literal
ratio set and a logarithmic spread control.

link::Classes/PitchRegisterOsc:: remembers recent scale degrees sample
coherently and turns every register position into an oscillator voice.

subsection:: Shared conventions

Every oscillator class implements code::.ar:: and deliberately rejects
code::.kr::.
Initialization-rate integers and fixed array sizes are checked by the language
classes and again by the server plugin where relevant. PM phase values and
phase offsets are expressed in radians; oscillator accumulation is internal
cycles. Signed frequency reverses phase motion.

Every public code::.ar:: ends with code::mul:: and code::add::. Multi-output
classes apply them independently after the native calculation.

Waveform indices, where a class offers four shapes, are code::0:: sine,
code::1:: triangle, code::2:: saw, and code::3:: pulse.

The code::oversample:: argument is fixed at construction and normally accepts
code::1::, code::2::, or code::4::. QuadratureFeedbackOsc also accepts
code::8::. Oversampling increases CPU approximately with the factor and is
most useful for nonlinear or modulation-rich patches.

subsection:: Integrated example

The buffer setup runs in a Routine and synchronizes the server before the
Synth starts.

code::
(
Routine({
    var scaleBuffer = ScaleSpreadOsc.prepareScale(
        s, [1, 9/8, 5/4, 4/3, 3/2, 5/3, 15/8]
    );
    s.sync;

    {
        var root = 38;
        var scaleLayer = ScaleSpreadOsc.ar(
            scaleBuffer, root,
            center: LFNoise2.kr(0.12).range(-7, 7),
            spread: LFTri.kr(0.035).range(0.3, 1.8),
            voices: 10, modulation: 0.15, stereo: 0.9
        );
        var harmonicLayer = Splay.ar(
            HarmonicStrideOsc.ar(
                root * 2,
                LFNoise1.kr(0.18).range(0.5, 2.5),
                0.9
            )
        );
        var partialLayer = PartialClusterOsc.ar(
            root, partials: 180,
            warp: LFNoise1.kr(0.08).range(-0.12, 0.18),
            tilt: 1.4, combPeriod: 7, combDepth: 0.55,
            voices: 3, detune: 0.07,
            maxPartials: 256
        );
        var signal = (scaleLayer * 0.45)
            + (harmonicLayer * 0.2)
            + (partialLayer * 0.25);
        Limiter.ar(LeakDC.ar(signal), 0.8, 0.01) * 0.08
    }.play;
}).play;
)
::

